/// EXPECTED TO WORK — currently REJECTED. (iterators stress test, 2026-07-14)
///
/// Writing the type of a function over a USER-DEFINED GENERIC type requires naming the type
/// parameter in the annotation — `def next(it: Iter(a)) = ...`. That is the natural, documenting
/// signature for every iterator combinator (`map : Iter(a) -> (a -> b) -> Iter(b)`, etc.).
///
/// ACTUAL: rejected with
///   CDZ0203: `Iter` is a type constructor — its type argument must be a type, but a value appears here
///   CDZ0101: unbound name `a`
/// The lowercase `a` in the annotation is not treated as a bound type variable, so a generic
/// signature over a user type constructor cannot be written at all.
///
/// WORKAROUND THAT WORKS (used by src/iter.cdz): leave the params UNANNOTATED — Cadenza infers the
/// generic type from use, so `def next(it) = match it with ...` compiles and monomorphizes correctly
/// (verified: the same body with unannotated params runs). The corpus's other idiom is an explicit
/// value-level `(: t Type)` binder (`def unbox(t: Type, b: Box(t)) = ...`), which is verbose and did
/// not compose cleanly when combined with an unforced/lazy element position.
///
/// DESIRED: either (a) accept a lowercase name in a user-generic annotation as an implicit universally
/// quantified type variable (the same "lowercase-payload-is-a-type-parameter" rule already used in
/// `type` declarations — see db.rs:453), so `Iter(a)` in an annotation binds `a`; or (b) give a
/// diagnostic that points at the working spellings (drop the annotation, or use a `Type` parameter)
/// rather than a bare "unbound name `a`".
///
/// This is a diagnostic/surface gap, NOT a blocker — the iterator library is written with unannotated
/// generic signatures. Filing so the annotation surface for user generics gets fixed.

type Iter =
  | FromList(List(a))
  | MapI(Tuple((a -> a), Iter(a)))

/// The intended signature — REJECTED today. (The working file omits the `: Iter(a)` annotation.)
def next(it: Iter(a)) = match it with
  | Iter.FromList(xs) => (match xs with
    | [] => Option.None(unit)
    | [h, .. t] => Option.Some((h, Iter.FromList(t))))
  | Iter.MapI(mf) => (match mf with | (f, src) => (match next(src) with
    | Option.None(_) => Option.None(unit)
    | Option.Some(p) => (match p with | (v, rest) => Option.Some((f(v), Iter.MapI((f, rest)))))))

def main() = 0
export { main }
